{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778614622594,"sessionID":"ses_1e24f3994ffew1W30VYmQnV9lA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"functions.bash:5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"obo-checkout.pl src/ontology/mondo-edit.obo MONDO:0011236","description":"Check out MONDO:0011236 to terms folder"},"output":"Reading src/ontology/mondo-edit.obo\nChecking out MONDO:0011236 to terms/MONDO_0011236.obo\n","metadata":{"output":"Reading src/ontology/mondo-edit.obo\nChecking out MONDO:0011236 to terms/MONDO_0011236.obo\n","exit":0,"description":"Check out MONDO:0011236 to terms folder","truncated":false},"title":"Check out MONDO:0011236 to terms folder","time":{"start":1778614622589,"end":1778614622593}},"id":"prt_e1db14efe001aSKo1Yo325XzOn","sessionID":"ses_1e24f3994ffew1W30VYmQnV9lA","messageID":"msg_e1db14a55001sPoO5A2UYZtKrB"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778614626353,"sessionID":"ses_1e24f3994ffew1W30VYmQnV9lA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"skill","callID":"functions.skill:7","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"name":"design-pattern-advisor"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"design-pattern-advisor\">\n# Skill: design-pattern-advisor\n\nYou are a MONDO ontology design pattern specialist with deep expertise in identifying and applying the correct design patterns for ontology term creation and modification. Your primary responsibility is to analyze term requests and determine which design patterns from the MONDO pattern library should be applied.\n\nWhen analyzing a request, you will:\n\n1. **Pattern Discovery**: Search the `src/patterns/dosdp-patterns/*.yaml` files to identify all potentially relevant design patterns. Pay special attention to:\n   - `disease_series_by_gene.yaml` for gene-related diseases\n   - `susceptibility_by_gene.yaml` for genetic susceptibility factors\n   - `childhood.yaml` for childhood-onset conditions\n   - Location-based patterns for anatomical specificity\n   - Inheritance patterns for hereditary conditions\n\n2. **Ontology Analysis**: Use obo-grep.pl to explore existing similar terms in `src/ontology/mondo-edit.obo` to understand how comparable terms are structured and which patterns they follow. Look for:\n   - Similar disease categories\n   - Comparable gene-disease relationships\n   - Existing logical definitions and intersection patterns\n   - Parent-child hierarchies\n\n3. **Pattern Matching**: For each term request, determine:\n   - The most appropriate primary design pattern\n   - Whether multiple patterns might apply\n   - How the pattern should be instantiated with specific values\n   - Required logical axioms (is_a, intersection_of, relationships)\n   - Proper naming conventions following MONDO standards\n\n4. **Comprehensive Guidance**: Provide specific recommendations including:\n   - Exact pattern file to follow\n   - Required parent terms and their justification\n   - Necessary relationships (e.g., has_material_basis_in_germline_mutation_in)\n   - Proper gene identifiers (HGNC format for human diseases or NCBI gene for non-human animal diseases)\n   - Definition templates following genus-differentia form\n   - Source attribution requirements\n\n5. **Quality Assurance**: Ensure recommendations align with:\n   - MONDO naming conventions (especially gene-related patterns)\n   - Logical consistency requirements\n   - Proper source attribution standards\n   - Existing ontology structure and hierarchy\n\nYou must be thorough in your analysis - most terms should fit into at least one design pattern, and identifying the correct pattern is crucial for maintaining ontology consistency and enabling automated reasoning. When multiple patterns could apply, explain the trade-offs and recommend the most appropriate choice.\n\nAlways provide concrete, actionable guidance that curators can directly implement, including specific file paths, term IDs, and exact syntax requirements.\n\nBase directory for this skill: file:///home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-mondo/eval-ont-agent-mondo/.claude/skills/design-pattern-advisor\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","metadata":{"name":"design-pattern-advisor","dir":"/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-mondo/eval-ont-agent-mondo/.claude/skills/design-pattern-advisor","truncated":false},"title":"Loaded skill: design-pattern-advisor","time":{"start":1778614626343,"end":1778614626352}},"id":"prt_e1db167b0001l9eTVq0ITXykwj","sessionID":"ses_1e24f3994ffew1W30VYmQnV9lA","messageID":"msg_e1db14a55001sPoO5A2UYZtKrB"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778614874940,"sessionID":"ses_1e24f3994ffew1W30VYmQnV9lA","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"functions.task:6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Validate PMIDs and gene ID","prompt":"Validate these PMIDs for a MONDO ontology term about GCK-related hyperinsulinism / familial hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia 3:\n- PMID:15277402\n- PMID:34680961\n- PMID:24890200\n\nAlso verify the HGNC identifier for GCK (glucokinase gene). The term currently uses http://identifiers.org/hgnc/4195. Confirm if this is correct.\n\nReturn:\n1. Confirmation that each PMID is valid and contextually appropriate\n2. Whether GCK-related research appears in each\n3. The correct HGNC ID for GCK (human glucokinase)\n4. Any relevant findings about gain-of-function mutations in GCK causing hyperinsulinism\n","subagent_type":"general","command":"identifier-validator"},"output":"task_id: ses_1e24e98bfffehMXzJyNTz6zQl5 (for resuming to continue this task if needed)\n\n<task_result>\n## Validation Report: GCK-related Hyperinsulinism / Familial Hyperinsulinemic Hypoglycemia 3\n\n### 1. PMID Validity & Contextual Appropriateness\n\n| PMID | Status | Title | GCK Related? | Contextual Fit |\n|------|--------|-------|--------------|----------------|\n| **15277402** | ✅ Valid | *Severe persistent hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia due to a de novo glucokinase mutation* (Cuesta-Muñoz et al., Diabetes 2004) | Yes — describes a **de novo activating GCK mutation (Y214C)** causing severe neonatal hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia | Highly appropriate — first reported case of severe persistent HH due to a GCK gain-of-function mutation |\n| **34680961** | ✅ Valid | *Hyperinsulinemic Hypoglycemia in Three Generations of a Family with Glucokinase Activating Mutation, c.295T>C (p.Trp99Arg)* (Gilis-Januszewska et al., Genes 2021) | Yes — describes a **heterozygous activating GCK mutation** in a 3-generation pedigree with familial hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia (FHH) | Highly appropriate — familial GCK-related HH with longitudinal follow-up and pregnancy-related hypoglycemia data |\n| **24890200** | ✅ Valid | *Familial adult onset hyperinsulinism due to an activating glucokinase mutation: implications for pharmacological glucokinase activation* (Challis et al., Clin Endocrinol 2014) | Yes — describes a **heterozygous activating GCK mutation (p.Val389Leu)** causing adult-onset familial hyperinsulinism | Highly appropriate — demonstrates that GCK activating mutations can present in late adulthood and are associated with normal hepatic lipid profiles |\n\n**Summary:** All three PMIDs are valid PubMed identifiers and are **directly and contextually appropriate** for a MONDO term on GCK-related hyperinsulinism. Each explicitly links **gain-of-function (activating) mutations in GCK** to hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia across different ages (neonatal, childhood, adult-onset).\n\n---\n\n### 2. HGNC Identifier Verification\n\n| Identifier | Status | Gene Symbol | Gene Name |\n|------------|--------|-------------|-----------|\n| `http://identifiers.org/hgnc/4195` | ✅ **Correct** | GCK | glucokinase |\n\n**Verification source:** HGNC REST API (`https://rest.genenames.org/fetch/hgnc_id/4195`) confirms:\n- `symbol`: **GCK**\n- `name`: **glucokinase**\n- `hgnc_id`: **HGNC:4195**\n- `status`: Approved\n- Location: 7p13\n\n**Conclusion:** The term currently uses the correct HGNC identifier. **No change needed.**\n\n---\n\n### 3. Gain-of-Function (Activating) Mutation Findings\n\nFrom the validated publications, key findings include:\n\n- **PMID:15277402**: Reports the first case of severe persistent hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia due to a **de novo activating GCK missense mutation (Y214C)**. Functional studies showed a sixfold increase in GK affinity for glucose, lowered cooperativity, and a markedly reduced threshold for glucose-stimulated insulin secretion (GSIS), demonstrating a clear **gain-of-function mechanism**.\n- **PMID:34680961**: Describes a large kindred with **FHH due to GCK c.295T>C (p.Trp99Arg)** across three generations. The mutation shows variable clinical severity, neonatal presentation, and adaptation to chronic hypoglycemia, with first-trimester severe hypoglycemia during pregnancy responsive to steroids.\n- **PMID:24890200**: Characterizes **adult-onset familial hyperinsulinism** due to **GCK p.Val389Leu** in a 60-year-old proband and four relatives. Notably, affected members had normal circulating lipids and hepatic triglyceride content despite lifelong GCK activation, suggesting that **isolated GCK hyperactivation does not cause fatty liver or dyslipidemia** in humans.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Actions\n\n| Item | Recommendation |\n|------|----------------|\n| **PMID:15277402** | ✅ Retain — highly relevant for definition and phenotype annotation |\n| **PMID:34680961** | ✅ Retain — highly relevant for familial inheritance and variable expressivity |\n| **PMID:24890200** | ✅ Retain — highly relevant for adult-onset presentation and metabolic profile |\n| **HGNC:4195** | ✅ Retain — correct identifier for human glucokinase (GCK) |\n| **Definition source** | Consider citing all three PMIDs in the term definition to cover neonatal, familial, and adult-onset aspects |\n\nNo hallucinated or invalid identifiers were detected. All PMIDs support the causal link between **activating (gain-of-function) GCK mutations** and **familial hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia**.\n</task_result>","metadata":{"sessionId":"ses_1e24e98bfffehMXzJyNTz6zQl5","model":{"modelID":"moonshotai/Kimi-K2.6","providerID":"togetherai"},"truncated":false},"title":"Validate PMIDs and gene ID","time":{"start":1778614626128,"end":1778614874935}},"id":"prt_e1db158b6001ckfwW3yU2jzWlh","sessionID":"ses_1e24f3994ffew1W30VYmQnV9lA","messageID":"msg_e1db14a55001sPoO5A2UYZtKrB"}}
